    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d576">

                <pb facs="0939" n="803" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg803"/>

                <head>No. 576.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Sir L. S. Sackville
                        West.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-05-04"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 4,
                        1888</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of the 2d instant, communicating a copy of Lord Salisbury’s
                    telegram to you <pb facs="0940" n="804" xml:id="pg804"/> of the 1st instant, to
                    the effect that the Government of Newfoundland has not forbidden the sale of
                    bait to American vessels, that several have already been supplied, and that
                    licenses are therefore unnecessary.</p>
                <p>I beg to renew the expression of satisfaction contained in my personal note of
                    the 1st instant, and trust the amicable disposition manifested may be productive
                    of good results to the relations of the two countries.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
